    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>A sultanate founded by Turkic slave soldiers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#samanid-empire">Samanids</a>, ruling from Ghazni over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>, Khurasan and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#punjab">Punjab</a>.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mahmud-of-ghazni">Mahmud of Ghazni</a> (r. 998–1030) led repeated raids into northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#india">India</a>, sacking the temple of Somnath in 1026.</li><li>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a> took Khurasan at Dandanaqan in 1040; the Ghurids burned Ghazni and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#lahore">Lahore</a> in 1186.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The scholar al-Biruni, brought to Ghazni after Mahmud conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#khwarazm">Khwarazm</a>, learned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> and wrote a detailed study of Indian religion, science and customs, completed around 1030.</p></aside>
    </section>

